<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Pronsk</h1><p class="st-meta">1186 CE – 1483 CE (approx.) · capital Pronsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An appanage principality of the Ryazan land whose princes repeatedly challenged the grand princes of Ryazan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pronsk, on the Pronya river south of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Ryazan</a>, was a fortified frontier town facing the steppe. In the 1180s it became the seat of a separate branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> princely house, and for three centuries its rulers held it as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the Grand Principality of Ryazan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pronsk princes often fought their <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Ryazan</a> cousins for the senior throne, sometimes with the help of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. In 1408 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-vladimirovich-of-pronsk">Ivan Vladimirovich of Pronsk</a> briefly drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> grand prince with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> support. Like other border lands, Pronsk suffered heavily from steppe raids and was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1237.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 15th century Pronsk was gradually reabsorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Ryazan</a>, and by the 1480s its princes had lost their independent role. The territory followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> in 1521, while the Pronsky princely family served in the Russian nobility.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-pronsk.jpg" alt="Principality of Pronsk" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Makakaaaa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_Oka_Principalities_(13th-14th_century).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1186 CE – 1483 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pronsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ivan Vladimirovich of Pronsk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pronsk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1334&amp;focus=principality-of-pronsk" aria-label="Principality of Pronsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5556 321 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Pronsk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5876 577L5875 563"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5875 563h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5876 577h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5876 577h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pronsk. <a href="/atlas?year=1334&amp;focus=principality-of-pronsk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
